# Network Scanner (Experimental Project)

## Overview

This project is a low-level network scanner written in C. It is designed to explore basic concepts of packet capture, TCP port probing, and local subnet discovery using raw sockets and system-level networking APIs on Linux.

This is **not a production-ready tool**. It is purely educational and experimental.

## Features

* Local network discovery (subnet detection)
* Host availability checking (basic TCP connect probing)
* Simple port scanning using a predefined list of common services
* Raw packet inspection using AF_PACKET sockets

## Important Notes

This project is **not optimized and not stable for real-world use**.

It has several limitations:

* Requires **root privileges** to run due to raw socket usage
* Relies on low-level packet capture which may behave inconsistently across networks
* Performance is not optimized (sequential scanning, no concurrency)
* Network discovery logic is experimental and may not work on all interface types

## Requirements

* Linux system
* GCC or compatible C compiler
* Root access (or CAP_NET_RAW capability)

To run:

```bash
sudo ./main
```

## Disclaimer

This tool is intended for **educational purposes only**, to understand:

* How TCP connections are established
* How raw sockets work in Linux
* Basic network scanning techniques

Do not use it on networks you do not own or have permission to test.

## Future Improvements

* Replace raw packet-based discovery with system-based subnet detection (`getifaddrs` or `ioctl`)
* Add multithreading for faster scanning
* Improve error handling and portability
* Reduce or eliminate root dependency where possible

---

**Status:** Experimental / Work in Progress


